From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bart Schilperoort <b.schilperoort@esciencecenter.nl>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 13:13:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] T5.2: device-resident coupling

With the GPU engine, apply_stresses! and collect_outputs! run as KernelAbstractions
kernels on the engine's device arrays (ModflowGPU get_value_ptr(...; device = true)),
with the record -> cell maps on the device (src/device_coupling.jl, DeviceCoupling).
River stages are written as offsets from the cell reference (bottom_bankfull - r on the
host in Float64, plus the Manning depth in T); groundwater_depth is top' - h' on the
device. Inputs are copied to the device in the BMI setters; the host copies of head,
baseflow and capillary are refreshed when read (getproperty), so an update moves no
array between host and device. The engine keeps no host mirrors (one-time reads through
ModflowGPU.get_value; package_flows too).

[solver] coupling = "auto" | "device" | "host" ("auto" = device for the GPU engine; host
keeps the T5.1 mirror path; mf6 only host). BMI.get_value_ptr(m, name; device = true)
returns the device arrays (heads as offsets from head_reference(m)). KernelAbstractions
as a direct dependency (Manifest.toml re-resolved). Tests: device vs host coupling in
single and double, no engine mirrors and no output copied during update, device stage
offsets and RCH rates, device depth and outputs vs the host values.
